 #### Neural Architecture Search for NeRF
 - **Steps:** Train NeRFs with different combinations of network depth and width on a volumetric video frame. Measure the quality of images rendered from the trained NeRFs using Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) [S6].
 - **Parameters:** Network Depth ($d$): Number of layers. Initial search space: {1, 2, 4, 6, 8}. Final selected space: {2, 4}. Network Width ($w$): Number of neurons in each layer. Initial search space: {64, 128, 256}. Final selected space: {128, 256} [S6].
-- **Metrics:** On RedAndBlack Frame #1450, PSNR was measured for various architectures to determine the optimal search space [S6].
+- **Numbers:** On RedAndBlack Frame #1450, PSNR was measured for various architectures to determine the optimal search space [S6].
 - **Comparison:** Network depth provides the ability to learn hierarchical representations, while network width provides the capacity to 'memorize' the training data. Reducing width monotonically decreases quality, while reducing depth first improves then drops quality if smaller than 2 [S6].
 
 ## Background
-The standard NeRF architecture was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1][S3, unofficial]. It uses two MLPs (coarse and fine) with positional encoding to map 3D coordinates and viewing directions to density and color. Subsequent works have modified this architecture to address limitations in speed, generalization, dynamic scenes, and semantic understanding. Key early modifications include NeRF-VAE (2021) for generative modeling [S1], and various approaches to sparse view synthesis and few-shot learning. More recent works have integrated Transformers [S1], SDFs [S1], and discrete representations like Voxels and Gaussians [S1]. The field continues to evolve with methods for dynamic scenes [S1], lighting decomposition [S1], and interactive editing [S1].
+The original implementation by Mildenhall et al. [1] employed hierarchical rendering to improve computational efficiency, using two networks (coarse and fine) to guide sampling point selection [S1]. Subsequent works have focused on accelerating training and inference, improving geometry, and extending NeRF to new domains such as dynamic scenes, human avatars, and generalizable synthesis. The field has evolved from simple MLP-based density fields to complex architectures incorporating transformers, SDFs, voxel grids, and physics-based simulations.
 
